<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Protest and freedom · 1905 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Dhana Dhanya Pushpa Bhara</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="bn">ধনধান্য পুষ্প ভরা</bdi> / <bdi lang="bn-Latn">Dhana Dhanya Pushpa Bhara</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Bengali song of 1905 in praise of the land of Bengal, written when the British split the province in two. It is still much loved.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Dwijendra Lal Roy</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Dwijendra Lal Roy</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Bengalis</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bengali</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Bengal (present-day India and Bangladesh)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1905</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Dwijendra Lal Roy, 1905; included in his play Shajahan, 1907)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhana-dhanya-pushpa-bhara">Dhana Dhanya Pushpa Bhara</a>&quot; (&quot;Lavished with a wealth of grains and flowers&quot;) is a patriotic song in Bengali composed in 1905 by the writer Dwijendra Lal Roy. On 20 July 1905 the British colonial government split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> Presidency in two along communal lines, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, with a Muslim majority, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> majority. The partition set off a nationalist surge, above all among Bengali Hindus, who held the move to be politically motivated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> such as this one, written in the same months, were meant to revive the spirit of a united Bengal and to raise public feeling against the communal divide. Roy put the song into his play Shajahan in 1907, and his son Dilip Kumar Roy later made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> version of it.</p><p>The song remains one of the most popular Bengali patriotic songs. It was among the songs that encouraged the freedom fighters in the Liberation War of Bangladesh, and when the Constitution of Bangladesh was drafted in 1972 it was one of the two songs proposed for the national anthem; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>'s &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a>&quot;, also written in the context of the partition of 1905, was chosen. In a 2006 survey by BBC Bangla it ranked tenth among the twenty greatest Bengali songs of all time. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> in Bangladesh, on 3 August 2024, thousands of protesters sang it in chorus.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic song of love for the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, rich in grain and flowers, written to call Bengalis to the unity of their divided province; proud and tender in mood.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Bengali</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Bengali</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ধনধান্য পুষ্পভরা আমাদের এই বসুন্ধরা</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">dhanadhānya puṣpabharā āmādera ei basundharā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Full of wealth, of grain and flowers, is this earth of ours;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তাহার মাঝে আছে দেশ এক–সকল দেশের সেরা;–</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tāhāra mājhe āche deśa eka–sakala deśera serā;–</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in its midst there is one land—the best of all lands;—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও সে, স্বপ্ন দিয়ে তৈরি সে-দেশ, স্মৃতি দিয়ে ঘেরা;</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o se, svapna diẏe tairi se-deśa, smr̥ti diẏe gherā;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O, it is made of dreams, that land, ringed round with memories;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">এমন দেশটি কোথাও খুঁজে পাবে নাকো তুমি,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">emana deśaṭi kothāo khum̐je pābe nāko tumi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>such a land you will find nowhere, search as you may,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও সে সকল দেশের রানি সে যে–আমার জন্মভূমি।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o se sakala deśera rāni se ye–āmāra janmabhūmi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O, she is the queen of all lands—she is the land of my birth.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">চন্দ্র সূর্য গ্রহ তারা, কোথায় উজল এমন ধারা।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">candra sūrya graha tārā, kothāẏa ujala emana dhārā.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Moon, sun, planets, stars—where do they shine in such a way?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">কোথায় এমন খেলে তড়িৎ এমন কালো মেঘে!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">kothāẏa emana khele taṛit emana kālo meghe!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where does the lightning play like this, in clouds as black as these!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তার পাখির ডাকে ঘুমিয়ে উঠি, পাখির ডাকে জেগে;</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tāra pākhira ḍāke ghumiẏe uṭhi, pākhira ḍāke jege;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the calls of her birds I rise from sleep, waking to the calls of birds;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">এমন দেশটি কোথাও খুঁজে পাবে নাকো তুমি,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">emana deśaṭi kothāo khum̐je pābe nāko tumi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>such a land you will find nowhere, search as you may,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও সে সকল দেশের রানি সে যে–আমার জন্মভূমি।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o se sakala deśera rāni se ye–āmāra janmabhūmi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O, she is the queen of all lands—she is the land of my birth.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">এত স্নিগ্ধ নদী কাহার, কোথায় এমন ধূম্র পাহাড়!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">eta snigdha nadī kāhāra, kothāẏa emana dhūmra pāhāṛa!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whose are rivers so gentle, where are hills so misty!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">কোথায় এমন হরিৎক্ষেত্র আকাশতলে মিশে!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">kothāẏa emana haritkṣetra ākāśatale miśe!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where do such green fields melt into the foot of the sky!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">এমন ধানের উপর ঢেউ খেলে যায় বাতাস কাহার দেশে!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">emana dhānera upara ḍheu khele yāẏa bātāsa kāhāra deśe!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In whose land does the wind play like this in waves over the rice!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">এমন দেশটি কোথাও খুঁজে পাবে নাকো তুমি,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">emana deśaṭi kothāo khum̐je pābe nāko tumi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>such a land you will find nowhere, search as you may,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও সে সকল দেশের রানি সে যে–আমার জন্মভূমি।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o se sakala deśera rāni se ye–āmāra janmabhūmi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O, she is the queen of all lands—she is the land of my birth.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">পুষ্পে পুষ্পে ভরা শাখী; কুঞ্জে কুঞ্জে গাহে পাখি;</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">puṣpe puṣpe bharā śākhī; kuñje kuñje gāhe pākhi;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Trees full of flower upon flower; in bower upon bower the birds sing;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">গুঞ্জরিয়া আসে অলি পুঞ্জে পুঞ্জে ধেয়ে–</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">guñjariẏā āse ali puñje puñje dheẏe–</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>humming, the bees come rushing, swarm upon swarm—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">তারা, ফুলের উপর ঘুমিয়ে পড়ে ফুলের মধু খেয়ে;</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">tārā, phulera upara ghumiẏe paṛe phulera madhu kheẏe;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they fall asleep upon the flowers, having drunk the flowers' honey;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">এমন দেশটি কোথাও খুঁজে পাবে নাকো তুমি,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">emana deśaṭi kothāo khum̐je pābe nāko tumi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>such a land you will find nowhere, search as you may,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও সে সকল দেশের রানি সে যে–আমার জন্মভূমি।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o se sakala deśera rāni se ye–āmāra janmabhūmi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O, she is the queen of all lands—she is the land of my birth.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ভায়ের মায়ের এত স্নেহ কোথায় গেলে পাবে কেহ!–</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">bhāẏera māẏera eta sneha kothāẏa gele pābe keha!–</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where could anyone go and find such love of brother and of mother!—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও মা তোমার চরণ দুটি বক্ষে আমার ধরি,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o mā tomāra caraṇa duṭi bakṣe āmāra dhari,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Mother, I hold your two feet to my breast;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">আমার এই দেশেতে জন্ম–যেন এই দেশেতে মরি–</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">āmāra ei deśete janma–yena ei deśete mari–</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my birth was in this land—may I die in this land—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">এমন দেশটি কোথাও খুঁজে পাবে নাকো তুমি,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">emana deśaṭi kothāo khum̐je pābe nāko tumi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>such a land you will find nowhere, search as you may,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bn">ও সে সকল দেশের রানি সে যে–আমার জন্মভূমি।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bn-Latn">o se sakala deśera rāni se ye–āmāra janmabhūmi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O, she is the queen of all lands—she is the land of my birth.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Dwijendra Lal Roy (1905), as quoted in the Bengali Wikipedia. Text from <a href="https://bn.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E0%A6%A7%E0%A6%A8%E0%A6%A7%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%A8%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%AF_%E0%A6%AA%E0%A7%81%E0%A6%B7%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%AA_%E0%A6%AD%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BE_%E0%A6%86%E0%A6%AE%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%A6%E0%A7%87%E0%A6%B0_%E0%A6%8F%E0%A6%87_%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%B8%E0%A7%81%E0%A6%A8%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%A7%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ISO 15919).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: ISO 15919 as a strict transliteration of the Bengali letters, as on the site's other Bengali songs: the inherent vowel written wherever the script has no hasanta (āmādera, sung āmāder; deśa, sung deś); ব is b (basundharā, bātāsa), য় ẏ, য y, ড় ṛ, ং ṁ, ঁ m̐, ৃ r̥, ৎ t; the danda । is given as a full stop. The source's dashes and its ';–' are kept.</li><li>lines x.3 and x.5: 'o se', 'O, it / O, she': ও is a sung exclamation and se 'it, she' the land; the land is she where it is the queen (x.5).</li><li>line 1.5 and refrain: janmabhūmi, 'the land of (my) birth', often rendered 'motherland'. 'nāko' is an emphatic 'not'.</li><li>line 2.1: dhārā, 'manner, way' (also 'stream, flow'): 'where do they shine in such a way'.</li><li>line 2.3: as printed here the line reads 'ghumiẏe uṭhi', 'I rise from sleep', so both halves speak of waking to the birds.</li><li>line 3.1: snigdha, 'gentle, soft, cool, soothing'; dhūmra, 'smoke-coloured, misty'.</li><li>line 4.1: śākhī, 'tree' (literally 'that which has branches').</li><li>line 4.2: ali, the bee; puñje puñje, 'in heaps, in swarm upon swarm'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
